Verify the sub claim after checking the JWT signature. JWT signature verification only proves the request came from Pulumi Cloud, not from your environment, so any environment that knows your adapter URL could call it. The sub format is pulumi:environments:org:<org>:env:<project>/<env>, except for the legacy default project, whose subject omits the project (pulumi:environments:org:<org>:env:<env>). Use a check like this and return HTTP 403 on failure:

function verifySubClaim(sub, allowedOrg, allowedProject, allowedEnv) {
  const expected = `pulumi:environments:org:${allowedOrg}:env:${allowedProject}/${allowedEnv}`;
  if (sub !== expected) {
    throw new Error(`Unauthorized: sub "${sub}" does not match expected "${expected}"`);
  }
}

Call this after verifying the JWT signature, passing the decoded sub and the org/project/env your adapter is configured to serve.

Define the allowed sizes per environment in ENVIRONMENT_LIMITS, which sets maxInstances and allowedSizes for dev, staging, and prod. For example, dev only allows 'small', staging allows 'small' and 'medium', and prod allows 'small', 'medium', and 'large'. The validateCostConstraints function checks the requested size against the environment's allowedSizes and returns an error such as 'dev environment only allows sizes: small' when the size is not permitted. The CostControlledDatabase component calls this validation in its constructor and throws an error 'Cost constraint violations:' if any errors are returned, so the deployment fails before any resource is created.

export const ENVIRONMENT_LIMITS = {
  dev: { maxInstances: 2, allowedSizes: ['small'] as InstanceSize[] },
  staging: { maxInstances: 3, allowedSizes: ['small', 'medium'] as InstanceSize[] },
  prod: { maxInstances: 10, allowedSizes: ['small', 'medium', 'large'] as InstanceSize[] },
} as const;

export function validateCostConstraints(size: InstanceSize, environment: Environment): string[] {
  const errors: string[] = [];
  const limits = ENVIRONMENT_LIMITS[environment];
  if (!limits.allowedSizes.includes(size)) {
    errors.push(`${environment} environment only allows sizes: ${limits.allowedSizes.join(', ')}`);
  }
  return errors;
}

It does not cap the value. The CostControlledDatabase component looks up the config for the chosen size in INSTANCE_CONFIGS and compares args.storage against config.maxStorage. If the requested storage is greater than the maximum for that size, the constructor throws an error with the message '<size> size allows maximum <maxStorage> GB storage', for example 'small size allows maximum 100 GB storage', and the database is not created. For instance, small allows up to 100, medium up to 500, and large up to 1000 GB.

const config = INSTANCE_CONFIGS[args.size];

// Enforce storage limits
if (args.storage > config.maxStorage) {
  throw new Error(`${args.size} size allows maximum ${config.maxStorage} GB storage`);
}

Create a PolicyPack from @pulumi/policy that contains a mandatory policy named 'database-cost-control'. The policy uses validateResourceOfType to target aws.rds.Instance resources. It reads the Size and Environment values from the instance tags, and if both are present, it runs validateCostConstraints on them and calls reportViolation for each error returned. Because the enforcementLevel is 'mandatory', violations block the deployment.

import { PolicyPack, validateResourceOfType } from '@pulumi/policy';
import { InstanceSize, Environment, validateCostConstraints } from '../types/cost-controls';
import { aws } from '@pulumi/aws';

new PolicyPack('cost-control-policies', {
  policies: [{
    name: 'database-cost-control',
    description: 'Enforce cost controls on database instances',
    enforcementLevel: 'mandatory',
    validateResource: validateResourceOfType(aws.rds.Instance, (instance, args, reportViolation) => {
      const size = instance.tags?.Size as InstanceSize;
      const environment = instance.tags?.Environment as Environment;
      if (size && environment) {
        const errors = validateCostConstraints(size, environment);
        errors.forEach(error => reportViolation(error));
      }
    }),
  }],
});

After completing the OpenID Connect configuration between Pulumi Cloud and Google Cloud (see the OpenID Connect integration guide), validate it by running the pulumi env open command with your environment identifier: pulumi env open <org>/<project>/<environment>. Replace <org>, <project>, and <environment> with your Pulumi organization and environment identifier. The output should look similar to the following, showing the login details and the retrieved secrets:

{
  "gcp": {
    "login": {
      "accessToken": "ya29.....",
      "expiry": "2023-11-09T11:12:41Z",
      "project": 123456789,
      "tokenType": "Bearer"
    },
    "secrets": {
      "api-key": "my-api-key",
      "app-secret": "my-app-secret"
    }
  }
}
